\section*{Wall's Poincare Duality Group Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
An integral PD4 group is claimed to be finitely presented with finite classifying space but to admit no closed aspherical topological four-manifold realization, refuting dimension-four manifold realization. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} OpenAI claims a group $G$ with a finite presentation and an integral Poincaré complex, finite, oriented and aspherical, of dimension four $Q$ and
\[
H^i(G;\mathbb ZG)=0\ (i\ne4),\qquad H^4(G;\mathbb ZG)\simeq\mathbb Z
\]
with trivial right $G$-action in degree four, but with no closed aspherical topological four-manifold having fundamental group $G$. The trivial $\mathbb ZG$-module has a finite finitely generated free resolution. Thus the claimed counterexample is integral duality with a finite classifying space, not merely rational duality.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 305, 2026-09-24): A marked smooth chamber becomes a graph-type Poincare pair after attaching cells along a hyperbolic middle-homology basis, while its boundary forbids a marked graph filling. Reflection closes the pair into an aspherical complex. Any manifold realization would provide a sphere-marked stable product; capping and controlled destabilization recover the forbidden filling, contradicting the chamber obstruction.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 305, 2026-09-24): A finitely presented oriented integral PD4 group with a finite free resolution but no closed aspherical topological four-manifold model directly refutes the problem’s all-dimensions-at-least-three realization assertion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
